      "math_academy_id": "q-94015",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-10{y}^{′}+25y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-10{y}^{′}+25y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=16.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA=2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=16,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{5x}+5Bx{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=16$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n5A+B=16.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $A=2$ gives $B=6.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{5x}+6x{e}^{5x}.\n$$",
